===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act or result of [[#Verb|uplifting]] (in various senses).
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2019|month=October|author=Tony Miles; Philip Sherratt|title=EMR kicks off new era|journal=Modern Railways|page=58|text=The EMR Regional timetable improvements also include a significant '''uplift''' in Sunday services.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 February 10|title=Network News: Additional funds enable preparatory work for Ashington reopening|journal=RAIL|issue=924|page=8|text=This means that while initial funding will come from the public purse, landowners along the route will eventually pay back a share of the '''uplift''' in land values created by the new line.}}
# {{lb|en|geology}} A [[tectonic]] [[upheaval]], especially one that takes place in the process of [[mountain]] [[building]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1971|author=George Finiel Adams; Jerome Wyckoff|title=Landforms|page=143|passage=Recent '''uplift''' of the Maine and Oregon coasts has not been enough to "undrown" the larger valleys; the shorelines are still submergent.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|
series=Proceeding of the International School of Physics "Enrico Fermi"|
seriesvolume=course 85|
title=Earthquakes: Observation, Theory and Interpretation|
year=1982|
publisher=North-Holland Publishing Company|
page=517|
pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=kRY7AAAAQBAJ&pg=PA517&dq=%22downlift%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjmue2_9OGJAxXe4skDHVx0O0UQ6AF6BAgMEAI#v=onepage&q=%22downlift%22&f=false|
text=This is explained as an '''uplift''' in the coast at a rate of 16.2 mm/y in the period before 1900, a [[downlift]] of 2.8 mm/y in the period 1900-1906 and a rapid «sinking» of the coast at 22.7 mm/y in the 24 months before the earthquake.
}}
# {{lb|en|transport}} The [[pick up|picking up]] and [[load]]ing of [[goods]] to be [[transport]]ed by a [[mover]].
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} A [[brassiere]] that raises the [[breast]]s.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1950|author=Norman Lindsay|authorlink=Norman Lindsay|title={{w|Dust or Polish?}}|publisher=Angus and Robertson|location=Sydney|page=15|passage=Dressed in a light silk brassiere and scanties, which was all she wore under her frock, she was unkind enough to say, "I think those '''uplift''' fakes of yours are a mistake, Sadie dear. They raise false expectations in the amorous male." "But my breasts are all right," said Sadie, hurt, and pushing her breasts up from under to prove they were in the right place[.]}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} An increase in a [[fine]] or [[penalty]] due to aggravating circumstances.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2020|author=David Begg|title=Economics for Business|page=148|passage=The CMA fined the companies involved £3.4 million, which included '''uplifts''' for director level involvement.}}

